WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.712 --> 00:00:10.220
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.230
So this is our last video in
the chapter on neutrino physics.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.340
And we'll talk about mass scales
and the nature of the neutrino

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.880
particle very briefly.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.940
When we think about how we can
measure the neutrino masses,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.040
there's a number of
methods which come to mind.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.470
The first one is to just
look out into the universe

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.520
and try to understand
how much matter in total

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.940
could come from as a
source of neutrinos.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.510
And one has to make
assumptions about the model,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.330
the cosmological models at hand.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.980
But if I accept those potential
biases or model dependencies,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.740
one finds that there's
a potential reach

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:58.660
of this kind of measurements
of 20 to 50 MeV, millielectron

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.040
volts.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.320
And the current best
limits are in the order

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.950
of 0.1 to 1 electron volt.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.910
A second source, and I'll
talk more about this later,

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.310
is the study of
neutrinoless beta decay--

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.950
double beta decays.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.235
Here, the current best limits
are on the order of 0.2

00:01:18.235 --> 00:01:20.050
to 0.4 electron volts.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:25.840
And there's a chance to reach
20 to 50 millielectron volts.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.670
This kind of measurement
will also answer the question

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.770
whether or not the neutrino is
a direct particle or a Majorana

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:36.100
particle as we discussed
in earlier lectures.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.740
And then there is the
more classical approach

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.340
of measuring the
mass of an neutrino

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.070
from the end point
spectrum of beta decays.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.640
And so here the
current best limit

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.230
is from the Kartrin experiment.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.110
And I talk about it
in the next slide.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.190
And it's in the order
of one electron volt.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.480
And there's a potential reach
to go down to 40 millielectron

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:05.980
volt. So currently
the range of limits

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.210
is in the order of 1 electron
volt or a bit better,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.980
and when we'll be
able to go down

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.750
to limits in the order of 20
to 50 millielectron volts.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.700
So here is a cartoon of
how those measurements are

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.810
being conducted.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.490
One starts with tritium.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:30.600
And it uses beta decay.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.680
And this lecture overall
is a good first entry

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.680
into the nuclear physics program
where we discuss beta decays

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.650
and other nuclear
decays in more detail.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.720
What we find here is
that you find an electron

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.170
and the neutrino--
antineutrino in this case--

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.640
being emitted.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.440
And so the name
of the game is now

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.910
to measure the electron energy
as precisely as possible,

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:59.400
and then find a sensitivity off
the neutrino mass in the end

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.660
point spectrum.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.740
And those small differences here
in the end point spectrum then

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.120
that leads to understanding
of the mass of the neutrino

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.060
because the total
energy in the collision

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.630
needs to be preserved.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:20.570
And so the entire story here
is about how precisely can we

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:23.510
measure the energy
of the electron

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.450
in order to infer the
neutrino mass in that.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.290
And so the latest results
came out last year

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.710
from the Kartrin
experiment and shows

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:38.270
that the result is consistent
with a neutrino mass of 0,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.320
and that we can set an upper
limit at 90% confidence level.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:50.160
That electron neutrino is
of mass of 1.1 electron

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.795
volt. Just as a reminder,
we measure the mass

00:03:55.795 --> 00:03:57.670
of the electron neutrino
in this decay, which

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:02.970
is the sum of the
individual components, mass

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.740
eigenstate, which make
up the electron neutrino.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.430
To just have historical context
in this discussion here,

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.310
we find that this
latest result is

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.560
an improvement of the
order of factor of 2

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.209
compared to previous result
by other experiments, which

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:26.310
had a very similar job
to measure the electron

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.670
energy in beta decays,
in the end point

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.070
spectrum of beta decays.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.990
There's a new
approach, which has

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:39.280
been proposed by Joe
Formaggio here from MIT,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.340
which changes the way
the electron energy is

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.370
being measured.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:46.800
So the idea is to have the
decay happen in magnetic fields,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:53.250
and use the cyclotron
radiation of single electrons.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.530
So the advantage
here is that one

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.290
doesn't have to move
the electrons somehow

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:02.655
into a spectrometer, but
can immediately measure

00:05:02.655 --> 00:05:03.780
the energy of the electron.

00:05:07.715 --> 00:05:09.090
And the measurement
of the energy

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.850
then turns into a
measurement of the frequency

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.570
and basically measures
the cyclotron frequency

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.840
of the electron circling
around in a magnetic field.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.590
And so it turns out that
one moves the measurement

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.170
of the energy of the
electron into a measurement

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:26.310
of a frequency.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.810
And thus frequency
can be measured

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.040
with very, very high precision.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:36.000
So there's some hope that
this kind of measurement

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.060
lead to very, very
precise results

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.750
of the energy of the electron
and with that the mass

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.910
of the neutrino.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.140
So the last slide
here is now how can we

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.660
figure out whether or
not the neutrino has

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.360
Dirac or Majorana nature.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.910
And this can be done,
or the high sensitivity

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.600
comes from so-called
neutrinoless double

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.550
beta decays.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.790
So one starts with
nuclear decays

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:07.170
where two electrons are
emitted, but no neutrino.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:10.400
And so this requires
that in this process

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.380
there's a transition
which includes

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.610
the neutrino where the neutrino
has to be its own antiparticle.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:21.330
And that just means that the
neutrino is of Majorana nature.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:25.810
This is being done by measuring,
again, the energy spectrum.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.130
You typically have all kinds
of background contributions,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.990
but also backgrounds from double
beta decays with two neutrinos.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.172
So you see this spectrum here.

00:06:36.172 --> 00:06:38.130
And then you look at the
end point of this part

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:45.270
here and find that there is
this peak, a precise sharp peak

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.250
of the energies of
the two electrons.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.030
The issue is that forecasting
where this peak is

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.860
requires proper knowledge of
the dynamics inside the nuclei

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.020
here.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.800
And those measurements
are being conducted.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.300
There's many of them conducted
in various nuclear transitions

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.290
or decays.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:11.920
And they haven't yielded
a positive result yet.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.700
Research is still
going on on this end.

